<launch>

    <!-- =====================================================================
         TERRAIN MODEL PATH
         =====================================================================
         lcmine.world instantiates the terrain by URI - model://lc_mine,
         model://grass_plane, model://smaze_wall, model://sun_2 - and those
         models live in this package's world/mine directory.  package.xml only
         exports <pkg>/model to GAZEBO_MODEL_PATH, which holds the robot
         models, not the terrain, so nothing in this repository ever told
         Gazebo where the terrain was.

         It worked anyway because ~/.bashrc exported the directory by hand.
         That is a dependency on one developer's interactive shell, and the
         path it exported pointed at ~/jounal_comparison - a stale duplicate of
         this workspace, with a typo in its name.  Any run that did not source
         that .bashrc (a script, a CI job, a background campaign) got
         "Unable to find uri[model://lc_mine]" and a world with NO GROUND: the
         robot spawns at z and free-falls forever while x and y never change,
         which reads exactly like a robot that refuses to move.

         Declaring it here makes the launch self-contained.  optenv keeps
         whatever the environment already had, so an existing setup is not
         broken by this.
         ===================================================================== -->
    <env name="GAZEBO_MODEL_PATH"
         value="$(find rocker_bogie)/world/mine:$(optenv GAZEBO_MODEL_PATH)" />

    <!-- these are the arguments you can pass this launch file, for example paused:=true -->
    <arg name="paused" default="true" />
    <arg name="use_sim_time" default="true" />
    <arg name="gui" default="true" />
    <arg name="headless" default="false" />
    <arg name="debug" default="false" />

    <!-- =====================================================================
         RANDOM SEED
         =====================================================================
         Gazebo's noise models (the IMU, and the <noise> block of the Velodyne's
         <ray>) draw from ignition::math::Rand.  Left alone, gzserver seeds that
         generator from the system clock, so no run can ever be repeated: the
         sensor noise of a given run exists once and is gone.

         run_campaign.sh passes seed:=<run_id>, so run 1 / 2 / 3 get seeds
         1 / 2 / 3.  The runs still differ from each other - different seeds
         give different noise, which is what the reported standard deviation
         measures - but each one is now anchored and can be reproduced.

         seed:=0 restores clock seeding (gzserver treats 0 as "pick your own").
         ===================================================================== -->
    <arg name="seed" default="0" />

    <!-- We resume the logic in empty_world.launch, changing only the name of the world to be launched -->
    <include file="$(find gazebo_ros)/launch/empty_world.launch">
        <arg name="world_name" value="$(find rocker_bogie)/world/mine/lcmine.world" />
        <arg name="debug" value="$(arg debug)" />
        <arg name="gui" value="$(arg gui)" />
        <arg name="paused" value="$(arg paused)" />
        <arg name="use_sim_time" value="$(arg use_sim_time)" />
        <arg name="headless" value="$(arg headless)" />
        <arg name="extra_gazebo_args" value="--seed $(arg seed)" />
    </include>

    <!-- Load the URDF into the ROS Parameter Server (global, fuera del namespace) -->
    <!-- El diferencial por engranajes (junta gearbox) se quito el
         2026-09-13 junto con los cuatro args que lo activaban y lo
         ajustaban: volcaba el vehiculo al spawnear.  El diferencial es
         el varillaje, differential_linkage. -->

    <!-- Tope superior de los bogies rev_3/rev_4, en radianes.  SE QUEDA EN
         0.6.  Se probo 1.0 el 2026-09-10 y el vehiculo VOLCO en el metro 54:
         el balancin se enrolla hasta el tope nuevo y se aparca ahi 25 metros,
         con una rueda en el aire el 100 % del tiempo en pleno llano.  El tope
         no ataba, sostenia.  La nota larga, con el A/B completo, esta en
         ensamblajeurdf.xacro.  Los rockers tampoco se tocan. -->
    <arg name="bogie_upper" default="0.6" />
    <!-- Cuanto se BAJA el pivote del bogie, en metros.  0.0 = el modelo de
         siempre.  Es la palanca contra el enrollamiento por par de rueda:
         M_motor sobre el brazo escala con la altura del pivote sobre la
         huella (0.529 m hoy).  La nota larga, con los numeros medidos, esta
         en ensamblajeurdf.xacro. -->
    <arg name="bogie_drop" default="0.0" />
    <!-- Cuanto se ACORTA la caida vertical del brazo del rocker, en metros.
         0.170395 -el defecto- deja el pivote del rocker a la MISMA ALTURA que
         el del bogie (0.3515 m sobre los ejes) y la rama trasera horizontal,
         que es el dibujo de libro del mecanismo; baja el chasis y el CM otro
         tanto sin mover nada en x ni en y.  0.0 recupera la altura del CAD.
         La nota larga, con el techo y lo que no limita, esta en
         ensamblajeurdf.xacro.  OJO: spawn_z de mas abajo va emparejado. -->
    <arg name="rocker_drop" default="0.299477" />
    <!-- GEOMETRIC DIFFERENTIAL - a real beam and two rods, closed with
         SDFormat ball joints.  ON BY DEFAULT: it is the only one of the three
         differential implementations tried here that both constrains the
         rockers and leaves the vehicle upright.

         Measured 2026-08-18, driving at 0.3 m/s over the gallery floor:

                              no differential      geometric linkage
             chassis pitch    -13.06 deg sd 10.33   +1.80 deg sd 0.24
             chassis roll       9.30 deg sd  3.46    4.68 deg sd 0.51
             pivots L / R      14.82 / 15.10         2.63 / 2.07
             |phi_l + phi_r|   29.9 deg mean         4.70 deg mean
                               68.2 deg max         10.5 deg max

         The chassis now stays essentially level while driving, which is the
         mechanical claim this study is built on and which nothing in the model
         delivered before.  4.7 deg of residual is not the 0.003 deg the gearbox
         achieves and cannot be: a linkage with real inertia and compliant
         contacts is a coupling, not an algebraic constraint.  It is a sixfold
         improvement and it does not tip the vehicle over, which the gearbox
         does.

         NOTE FOR THE PAPER: every result collected before this date was taken
         with NO effective differential at all, whatever the configuration said.
         They have to be regenerated. -->
    <arg name="differential_linkage" default="true" />

    <param name="rocker_bogie/robot_description" command="xacro --inorder '$(find rocker_bogie)/urdf/ensamblajeurdf.xacro' differential_linkage:=$(arg differential_linkage) bogie_upper:=$(arg bogie_upper) bogie_drop:=$(arg bogie_drop) rocker_drop:=$(arg rocker_drop)" />

    <!-- Spawn the robot in Gazebo from the URDF -->
    <!-- Spawn pose - x, y and heading MUST match the other two platforms
         (see sim_config.yaml).  These were hardcoded here while the other two
         packages already exposed them as args. -->
    <arg name="spawn_x" default="33.5" />
    <arg name="spawn_y" default="0.2" />
    <!-- 2026-09-13: base_link va a la altura de los ejes y centrado, asi que
         spawn_z = 1.16 + 0.0548 y ya NO depende de rocker_drop.  Deja el apoyo
         a la misma altura que el husky. -->
    <arg name="spawn_z" default="1.1848" />
    <!-- BAJADA 2026-09-01, de 1.3 a 1.16.  Medido: esta plataforma
         reposa en z = 1.0956, asi que 1.3 dejaba 20 cm de caida.  Con
         el contacto a kp = 1e7 ese impacto hacia GIRAR al Husky
         16.4 grados sobre +z durante el aterrizaje, y esos grados se
         quedaban como condicion inicial de la corrida.  Ahora la
         caida son 6 cm: suficiente para no aparecer dentro del
         terreno, poco para golpear.

         BAJADA OTRA VEZ, de 1.16 a 0.99, al acortar el brazo del rocker.
         rocker_drop sube las seis ruedas 0.170395 m en el marco de base_link,
         asi que base_link reposa 0.170395 m mas abajo (1.0956 -> 0.9252) y
         spawn_z tiene que bajar lo mismo para conservar esos 6 cm.  Si se
         cambia rocker_drop hay que restarselo aqui: spawn_z = 1.16 -
         rocker_drop. -->
    <arg name="spawn_Y" default="0.0" />

    <!-- =====================================================================
         LIDAR: normal (el de siempre) o troceado.  Ver la nota completa en
         differential/launch/lcmine_husky_world.launch.
         =====================================================================
         Esta plataforma spawnea su robot_description, no un SDF, asi que el
         modelo troceado sale de pasar el xacro por el generador.  Se spawnea
         desde un parametro APARTE: robot_description sigue siendo el de
         siempre, para que gazebo_ros_control, los controladores y
         robot_state_publisher vean exactamente el mismo robot en los dos
         modos.  Lo unico que el rotor añade al modelo spawneado es una
         articulacion continua que nadie mas mira.
         ===================================================================== -->
    <arg name="lidar" default="normal" />

    <!-- Los args del xacro tienen que ir TAMBIEN aqui, y ser los mismos que
         arriba.  Sin ellos el generador invoca `xacro ensamblajeurdf.xacro` a
         secas y se lleva los defaults DEL XACRO, que no son los de este
         launch: differential_linkage=false (sin varillaje).  El modo
         sliced spawneaba asi un robot distinto del que describe
         robot_description, y distinto del que corre en modo normal: la
         comparacion entre los dos LiDAR medía tambien un cambio de
         suspension. -->
    <param name="swept_robot_description"
           if="$(eval arg('lidar') == 'swept')"
           command="$(find robot_metrics)/scripts/retopic_lidar.py --xacro
                    $(find rocker_bogie)/urdf/ensamblajeurdf.xacro
                    --xacro-arg differential_linkage:=$(arg differential_linkage)
                    bogie_upper:=$(arg bogie_upper)
                    bogie_drop:=$(arg bogie_drop)
                    rocker_drop:=$(arg rocker_drop)" />

    <node name="spawn_rocker_bogie" pkg="gazebo_ros" type="spawn_model"
          unless="$(eval arg('lidar') == 'swept')"
          args="-param rocker_bogie/robot_description -urdf -model rocker_bogie -x $(arg spawn_x) -y $(arg spawn_y) -z $(arg spawn_z) -Y $(arg spawn_Y)"
          output="screen" />

    <node name="spawn_rocker_bogie" pkg="gazebo_ros" type="spawn_model"
          if="$(eval arg('lidar') == 'swept')"
          args="-param swept_robot_description -urdf -model rocker_bogie -x $(arg spawn_x) -y $(arg spawn_y) -z $(arg spawn_z) -Y $(arg spawn_Y)"
          output="screen" />

    <!-- Arm swept: el LiDAR monolitico con la distorsion de barrido
         metida por formulas.  Ver sweep_distortion.launch. -->
    <arg name="cut_angle_deg" default="0.0" />
    <arg name="rotor_dir"     default="1" />
    <include file="$(find robot_metrics)/launch/sweep_distortion.launch"
             if="$(eval arg('lidar') == 'swept')">
        <arg name="model_name"    value="rocker_bogie" />
        <arg name="base_frame"    value="rocker_bogie/base_link" />
        <arg name="frame_id"      value="rocker_bogie/velodyne_base_link" />
        <arg name="cut_angle_deg" value="$(arg cut_angle_deg)" />
        <arg name="rotor_dir"     value="$(arg rotor_dir)" />
    </include>


    <group ns="rocker_bogie">

        <!-- Velocity controllers for 6 wheels -->
        <rosparam>
            joint_state_controller:
                type: joint_state_controller/JointStateController
                publish_rate: 50

            # =================================================================
            # WHEEL / STEERING JOINT MAP
            # =================================================================
            # Derived from forward kinematics of the URDF at zero configuration
            # (robot_metrics/scripts/extract_robot_specs.py --wheel-map), not
            # from the CAD export order.
            #
            # Frame: base_link is authored facing -x - the camera sits at
            # x = -0.07 with rpy yaw = pi, and the robot spawns at Y = pi.  With
            # z up and a right-handed frame, LEFT = z_hat x forward = -y_hat.
            # So in base_link coordinates FRONT is the smaller x and LEFT is
            # the smaller y.  Measured wheel centres:
            #
            #   nav position   wheel       x_bl[m]  y_bl[m]  drive   steer
            #   front-left     Rueda_6_1    -0.143   -0.182  rev_11  rev_5
            #   front-right    Rueda_1_1    -0.143   +0.414  rev_12  rev_7
            #   middle-left    Rueda_4_1    +0.283   -0.275  rev_10  (none)
            #   middle-right   Rueda_3_1    +0.283   +0.507  rev_13  (none)
            #   rear-left      Rueda_2_1    +0.710   -0.275  rev_9   rev_6
            #   rear-right     Rueda_5_1    +0.710   +0.507  rev_14  rev_8
            #
            # The STEERING map below was already correct and is unchanged.
            #
            # The DRIVE map was mirrored: every controller was bound to the
            # diametrically opposite wheel (fl->rev_14 is the rear-RIGHT wheel,
            # and so on), because the labels had been assigned in raw base_link
            # axes without accounting for the 180 deg model heading.  Front/rear
            # being swapped is harmless - the Ackermann solution gives front and
            # rear the same speed - but LEFT/RIGHT being swapped is not: in
            # every turn the inner-wheel speeds were sent to the outer wheels
            # and vice versa, so all six wheels were dragged sideways through
            # the corner.  That inflates the measured slip and the IMU
            # vibration, both of which the paper attributes to locomotion type.
            # =================================================================
            wheel_vel_controller_fl:
                type: velocity_controllers/JointVelocityController
                joint: rev_11
            wheel_vel_controller_fr:
                type: velocity_controllers/JointVelocityController
                joint: rev_12
            wheel_vel_controller_ml:
                type: velocity_controllers/JointVelocityController
                joint: rev_10
            wheel_vel_controller_mr:
                type: velocity_controllers/JointVelocityController
                joint: rev_13
            wheel_vel_controller_rl:
                type: velocity_controllers/JointVelocityController
                joint: rev_9
            wheel_vel_controller_rr:
                type: velocity_controllers/JointVelocityController
                joint: rev_14

            steer_controller_fl:
                type: position_controllers/JointPositionController
                joint: rev_5
            steer_controller_fr:
                type: position_controllers/JointPositionController
                joint: rev_7
            steer_controller_rl:
                type: position_controllers/JointPositionController
                joint: rev_6
            steer_controller_rr:
                type: position_controllers/JointPositionController
                joint: rev_8

            # Effort controllers for the two rocker pivots.  They exist ONLY so
            # that rocker_differential.py can apply the differential coupling
            # torque; a zero command leaves the pivot free, so the suspension
            # stays passive when that node is not running.
            rocker_diff_effort_left:
                type: effort_controllers/JointEffortController
                joint: rocker_pivot_left
            rocker_diff_effort_right:
                type: effort_controllers/JointEffortController
                joint: rocker_pivot_right

            gazebo_ros_control:
                # GAINS RESCALED WITH THE MASS MATCHING (mass_scale = 0.474039,
                # see robot_metrics/scripts/scale_masses.py). These loops close
                # effort onto inertia: for J*qdd = K*e the natural frequency is
                # sqrt(K/J), so halving every J and leaving K alone would have
                # raised the bandwidth by sqrt(2) and changed the tracking
                # behaviour of the Rocker-Bogie only - a new confound in place
                # of the one being removed. Multiplying K, I and D by the same
                # 0.474039 keeps wn and zeta exactly where they were tuned.
                # Native values were p:26/i:1 (wheels) and p:50/i:5/d:1 (steer).
                #
                # i_clamp IS LOAD-BEARING, do not drop it again.  control_toolbox
                # reads i_clamp_min / i_clamp_max off the parameter server and
                # DEFAULTS BOTH TO 0.0, so the i: 0.474 declared here did
                # nothing at all until this line existed: the integral term was
                # clamped to zero on every step and the loop was P-only.  That
                # is what made the platform sag on a sustained grade - the
                # steady-state droop had nothing to null it out.  The clamp is
                # the joint's own torque ceiling from the actuation budget
                # (sim_config.yaml), so integral action may use the full
                # authority the budget allows and no more.
                pid_gains:
                    rev_9:  {p: 10.225, i: 9.831, d: 0.0, i_clamp: 20.000}
                    rev_10: {p: 10.225, i: 9.831, d: 0.0, i_clamp: 20.000}
                    rev_11: {p: 10.225, i: 9.831, d: 0.0, i_clamp: 20.000}
                    rev_12: {p: 10.225, i: 9.831, d: 0.0, i_clamp: 20.000}
                    rev_13: {p: 10.225, i: 9.831, d: 0.0, i_clamp: 20.000}
                    rev_14: {p: 10.225, i: 9.831, d: 0.0, i_clamp: 20.000}
                    rev_5:              {p: 23.702, i: 2.370, d: 0.474}
                    rev_6:              {p: 23.702, i: 2.370, d: 0.474}
                    rev_7:              {p: 23.702, i: 2.370, d: 0.474}
                    rev_8:              {p: 23.702, i: 2.370, d: 0.474}
                    # NOTE: the passive suspension joints (rocker pivots and
                    # bogies) deliberately have NO pid_gains entry. A gain here
                    # would let gazebo_ros_control regulate them, which is what
                    # used to keep the suspension locked. The rocker pivots are
                    # effort-controlled, and an effort interface needs no PID.
        </rosparam>

        <!-- Load and start controllers -->
        <node name="controller_starter" pkg="controller_manager" type="controller_manager"
              args="spawn joint_state_controller wheel_vel_controller_fl wheel_vel_controller_ml wheel_vel_controller_rl wheel_vel_controller_fr wheel_vel_controller_mr wheel_vel_controller_rr steer_controller_fl steer_controller_fr steer_controller_rl steer_controller_rr rocker_diff_effort_left rocker_diff_effort_right"
              output="screen" />

        <!-- Rocker differential: enforces phi_left + phi_right = 0.
             Set enabled:=false for the "no differential" ablation. -->
        <node name="rocker_differential" pkg="rocker_bogie" type="rocker_differential.py" output="screen">
            <!-- SUPERSEDED, off by default.  Kept only so the old
                 soft-coupling behaviour can be reproduced for comparison:
                 enabled:=true with differential_linkage:=false gives the
                 pre-2026-08-18 configuration. -->
            <param name="enabled"   value="false" />
            <!-- Rescaled with the mass matching, same argument as the PID
                 gains above. The rocker subtree inertia about its pivot,
                 measured from the expanded URDF by the parallel axis theorem,
                 went from 8.535 to 4.046 kg*m^2 (25 links, 13.48 kg). Scaling
                 stiffness and damping by the same 0.474039 leaves the coupling
                 dynamics exactly where they were tuned:
                     wn   = sqrt(948.08/4.046) = 15.3 rad/s = 2.4 Hz
                     zeta = 28.44/(2*sqrt(948.08*4.046)) = 0.230
                 both identical to the native 2000/60 pair, and 2.4 Hz is far
                 below the node's 200 Hz update rate and the 1 kHz physics step.
                 Native values were 2000.0 / 60.0 / 400.0.

                 Worth knowing: zeta = 0.23 means the differential is markedly
                 underdamped and a step disturbance overshoots by roughly 48%
                 before settling. That is inherited from the original tuning,
                 not introduced here, and it is left alone so the mass matching
                 changes one thing only. If the constraint residual reported by
                 rocker_differential.py on shutdown turns out to be dominated by
                 ringing, critical damping is damping = 2*sqrt(k*I) = 123.9. -->
            <param name="stiffness" value="948.08" />
            <!-- DAMPING STAYS AT 28.44 (zeta = 0.23).  Two things are true
                 and only one of them justifies changing it:

                 (a) the tuning IS wrong.  rocker_differential.py computed the
                     zeta it prints from a hardcoded inertia of 0.45 kg*m^2
                     while the model's rocker subtree is 4.046, so it reported
                     ~0.69 every launch when the truth was 0.23.  That is fixed
                     - the inertia is a parameter now and the node reports
                     honestly.

                 (b) it is NOT what tilts the chassis.  The comment below
                     prescribed 2*sqrt(k*I) = 123.87 "if the residual turns out
                     to be dominated by ringing".  Measured 2026-08-18: it is
                     not.  Both pivots sit PINNED against their +-0.6 rad
                     (34.377 deg) stops with sd 0.25 deg - stationary, not
                     oscillating - so error_dot is ~0 and damping contributes
                     nothing.  Running at zeta = 1.000 changed the residual
                     from 66.8 to 68.3 deg and the pitch from -31.2 to -27.0
                     deg: no fix.

                 The real problem is geometric, see rocker_pivot_zero_note in
                 sim_config.yaml.  Changing a tuned parameter that feeds the
                 published stability numbers, on a premise that measurement has
                 disproven, would be the wrong trade. -->
            <param name="damping"   value="28.44" />
            <param name="inertia"   value="4.046" />
            <param name="max_torque" value="189.6" />
        </node>

        <!--
          Twist to individual wheel velocities and steering angles.

          GEOMETRY (corrected 2026-07).  All four values are measured from the
          URDF by extract_robot_specs.py, not guessed:

            wheelbase    front axle to rear axle      0.8532 m  (was 0.57)
            rear_track   middle/rear wheel separation 0.7821 m  (was 0.6775)
            front_track  front wheel separation       0.5961 m  (not modelled
                                                                before)
            wheel_radius cylinder collision radius    0.178  m  (correct)

          The old wheelbase was 33% short and the old track 13% short, so the
          Ackermann solution was computed for a vehicle that does not exist:
          every commanded yaw rate produced a steering angle that was too small
          and inner/outer wheel speeds that did not match the actual turn.  The
          robot understeered and scrubbed its way through the corners, which
          inflates both the slip metric and the IMU vibration signal that the
          paper attributes to the locomotion type.
        -->
        <node name="twist_to_wheels" pkg="rocker_bogie" type="twist_to_wheels.py" output="screen">
            <param name="wheelbase" value="0.7355" />
            <param name="front_track" value="0.4462" />
            <param name="rear_track" value="0.5757" />
            <param name="wheel_radius" value="0.148" />
            <!-- 1.1 rad, matching the joint limit: the point turn needs
                 1.0484 rad and clamping below that just scrubs.

                 PROBADO Y REVERTIDO 2026-08-24: se bajo a 0.7854 (45 deg)
                 para ver si el giro de las ruedas de esquina era lo que
                 desequilibraba a la plataforma en el escalon.  No se pudo
                 evaluar: move_base dejo de poder conducir.  El radio minimo
                 de la plataforma es half_wheelbase / tan(max_steer), o sea
                     1.1000 rad -> 0.217 m
                     0.7854 rad -> 0.427 m
                 y el planificador local no conoce ese limite: con
                 min_vel_x 0.0 y max_vel_theta 1.0, DWA puede pedir v=0.1 con
                 w=1.0, que son 0.10 m de radio.  Al doblarse el radio minimo
                 el robot dejo de seguir los arcos comandados y move_base
                 aborto tres veces seguidas por "oscillating over and over" y
                 "a valid control could not be found", tras agotar sus
                 recuperaciones; el cmd_vel se quedo en cero y la plataforma
                 parada.  Es el mismo desajuste que describe el comentario de
                 rev_5 en el URDF para el lock de 28.6 deg, en version suave.

                 Si se vuelve a intentar recortar la direccion, hay que
                 ajustar a la vez el planificador (subir min_vel_x o bajar
                 max_vel_theta hasta que v/w no baje del radio alcanzable) - y
                 eso es condicion compartida por las tres plataformas. -->
            <param name="max_steer_angle" value="1.1" />
            <!-- 0.0 for all three platforms; see command.min_angular_rad_s
                 in sim_config.yaml.  The tracked platform used to force 0.25. -->
            <param name="min_angular" value="0.0" />
        </node>

        <!-- convert joint states to TF transforms for rviz, etc -->
        <node name="robot_state_publisher" pkg="robot_state_publisher" type="robot_state_publisher" output="screen">
            <param name="tf_prefix" value="rocker_bogie" />
            <remap from="robot_description" to="/rocker_bogie/robot_description" />
        </node>

    </group>

    <!-- base_link_nav ya no existe: base_link mira al avance (2026-09-13). -->


    <!-- ================= COVARIANZAS DEL IMU =============================
         gazebo_ros_imu_sensor ata el ruido y la covarianza al mismo parametro
         (<gaussianNoise>): llena las tres diagonales con gaussianNoise^2.
         Aca el ruido tiene que salir del generador SEMBRADO -los bloques
         <noise> del <sensor>, que usan ignition::math::Rand y por tanto
         responden a la opcion seed de gzserver- y no al rand() de C del plugin,
         que la semilla no alcanza.  Por eso <gaussianNoise> esta en 0.0, y el
         efecto colateral fue que las covarianzas quedaron en cero, o sea
         "desconocidas" segun la spec de sensor_msgs/Imu.  RTAB-Map consume
         este topic.

         Solucion: el plugin publica crudo en /imu/data_raw y este nodo
         republica /imu/data con las diagonales puestas desde los stddev de
         sim_config.yaml.  Los consumidores (rtabmap, metrics_logger,
         rearing_guard, wheel_odom) siguen leyendo /imu/data sin cambios.
         Medido/aplicado 2026-08-23. -->
    <!-- AHRS.  Fusiona giroscopo y acelerometro para producir la orientacion que
         consumen rtabmap, metrics_logger y wheel_odometry.py.  Sustituye a
         imu_covariance.py el 2026-09-09: aquel solo rellenaba covarianzas y
         dejaba pasar la orientacion del plugin, que es la pose EXACTA del
         link.  No era verdad de terreno con ruido: la spec de SDF no tiene
         elemento de ruido para la orientacion, asi que `gzserver -seed` no
         tenia ahi nada que sembrar.  Ver la cabecera de imu_ahrs.py. -->
    <node name="imu_ahrs" pkg="robot_metrics" type="imu_ahrs.py"
          output="screen">
        <!-- Sin esto la salida de un nodo Python se queda en el buffer cuando
             roslaunch va redirigido a un archivo, que es como corre la
             campana: el nodo funciona pero su log no aparece hasta que sale.
             Aqui tapaba justo lo que hay que poder auditar, el sesgo del
             giroscopo que se estimo al arrancar. -->
        <env name="PYTHONUNBUFFERED" value="1" />
        <remap from="~input"  to="/imu/data_raw"/>
        <remap from="~output" to="/imu/data"/>
        <param name="angular_velocity_stddev"    value="0.009"/>
        <param name="linear_acceleration_stddev" value="0.021"/>
        <param name="orientation_stddev"         value="0.02"/>
        <param name="tau"                        value="1.0"/>
        <param name="accel_gate"                 value="0.15"/>
        <param name="bias_samples"               value="200"/>
    </node>

</launch>
